00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
the u.n security council has held an  emergency session to discuss the  taliban's restrictive policies on women  in afghanistan the uk envoy said it was  wrong and regressive that the hardline  group had banished women to the  sidelines of life the taliban scabs  range from prohibiting women from  traveling without a male chaperone  cutting them off from most employment  and banning older girls from going to  school measures designed to erase women  from public life the latest of these  dictates requires women to cover  themselves from head to toe  burka is not my identity  these afghan women chant it  asking for justice under the taliban  route takes a lot of courage but the  latest order for them to wear a complete  hijab meaning to cover up everything but  their eyes has anguished them once again  we want to live as living beings live as  noble creatures not to live as a captive  in one corner of the house not to be  held in the cage of our house and our  husbands go and back for food we never  want a day like this  like these usually won't last long  get out of here or i'll kill you this  armed law enforcer says  most of the women and girls have no  choice but to comply with the  restrictions the harshest since the  taliban came to power in august 2021  just a month after the takeover the  regime replaced the women's ministry  with the ministry of virtue and vice  women and girls have been told to stay  home  in the third quarter of 2021 their  employment levels fell by an estimated  16 percent much more than men  in december the taliban bent  long-distance travel for women without a  closed male relative  all of these happened while the de facto  authorities say they have changed  we have made progress in administration  in politics  in the development sector of the country  and in interaction with the nation  and the world  with each passing day  in all walks of life  we will gain more experience  and make more progress  progress is not what these girls  experienced  the taliban promised they could return  to secondary school in march  but they were sent home instead  a u-turn in policy that confirmed to  these girls that the taliban is  determined to strip them off their basic  rights  and joining me now from washington dc is  the u.s special envoy for afghan women  girls and human rights reena amiri  welcome mr miri the taliban continued to  issue draconian dictators curtailing  women's freedoms in afghanistan in your  assessment is the situation for women  and girls going to keep getting worse  well first thank you for having me on  and putting a spotlight on this really  critical issue  and unfortunately what we're seeing is  an expanding set of restrictions on  afghan women and it's quite systematic  um and it's it's certainly not looking  good as as one as one afghan woman  characterized it to me she said that she  had a sense that there was a taliban war  on women um and the this i think is is  something to to really take stock of is  that women in afghanistan feel under  siege by by the de facto authorities in  afghanistan  so how can you or the united states end  this siege  well we've made our  position emphatically clear that the  issue the situation of afghan women and  girls and human rights is of critical  importance to us  the taliban have consistently said that  they no longer want to be a pariah uh  be seen as a pariah by the international  community they want to have a  relationship with the international  community where they're seen as as  respected counterparts and we have said  that so long as you continue to behave  in the same way that you did as the  1990s there's going to be no change in  our relationship and moreover  the uh you know there's many things that  the taliban want they want  greater economic engagement they want  greater normalization they want more  diplomatic engagement all of that is  contingent on their behavior and what  they do in regard to women girls and the  human rights situation in afghanistan  they know that yet they keep  walking back on commitments that they  had made before they took over a rule in  their country i mean the majority of  women still can't work they can't travel  without male guardians secondary  education is a no-go do the taliban  really care  the taliban should care not just because  of the international community but  because they cannot hold the peace they  cannot prevent further brain drain in  afghanistan uh they cannot make  afghanistan  an economically viable country  and prevent further economic collapse  when they when they deny 50 of the  population their rights  now on monday state department spokesman  ned price said the u.s has caught a  number of tools it can use if the  taliban don't undo some of their recent  decisions concerning women  what are these tools  we are we've had  ongoing conversations discussions with  the taliban and at a working level we  continue to engage them and to make very  clear that they are there are a set of  uh demands on the on the side of the  taliban there are things that they would  like to have happen and for that the  role of the us and the international  community are key  um they understand that and we as i said  we've made it explicit those are the uh  i'm sorry to interrupt man but they  don't seem to be listening they seem to  be hearing you but they don't seem to be  acting on it which is why you have one  decision after another so they know they  want recognition and they need to do a  certain number of things to get it but  they still  continue with these draconian dictats  unfortunately what appears clear is the  taliban are prioritizing  their internal cohesion over the uh the  fate of the uh the country of the fate  of the population and their relationship  with the international community  they are making decisions that i think  will end up  costing them significantly on many  different levels  now uh last year in an um interview in  september you had said that afghan women  feel uh caught a profound sense of  betrayal  uh after the u.s withdrawal from the  country  given that is what they feel in your  words should afghanistan's women and  girls expect anything at all from the  united states  well i would note that one of the uh in  response to  the concerns heard from afghan women and  you know many and at that point i was an  independent actor when i made those  comments in response to to that concern  the us government established my  position and my office  and has  provided a a  i think significant support to my office  to uh to address this issue um so i  think that the commitment is certainly  there i've been in my position for five  months and i've seen that throughout the  government there is uh broad-scale  support for my efforts to uh to advance  the situation of women and girls  all right we leave it there for today  thank you so much for joining us irina  amiri the u.s special envoy for afghan  women girls and human rights thank you  so much  thank you  some afghan women are trying to fight  back 20 female lawmakers have set up a  parliament in exile in greece they  escaped to the capital athens when the  taliban seized power last august they  are determined to use their influence  and experience to help the women left  behind in their homeland  three years ago nazifa yusufi beck was  elected to the afghan parliament now the  politician has been forced into exile in  athens she says there's so much she'd  wanted to get done  i got up every morning with the will and  the gold to fight for afghan society and  for women's rights  those efforts were brutally interrupted  when the taliban seized power last  august  crowds besieged kabul airport desperate  to leave the country yusufy beck was  among them she knew that if she didn't  leave afghanistan the taliban would hunt  her down  she escaped with her children but that  deeply scarred her  when we got here we had lost everything  we had to leave our family behind my  parents all of our belongings  i was in bad shape traumatized  but i kept forcing myself to hope  now we're doing much better in greece  but the poor people back in afghanistan  things are quite bad  every day yusufi beck talks to her  family and friends back home  she was shocked when one day after the  afghan new year in march she heard that  the taliban had broken their promise to  allow girls to go back to school  the girl she's chatting to here had been  desperate to return to a classroom she  was just about to graduate  we hope that the women who are now  abroad will fight for us and try to find  a way for us to go back to school  and they are yusufi beck is not alone in  athens  shagufa norzai is one of many other  afghan lawmakers in exile in the city  together with her former colleagues  norzai wants to fight for afghan women  from here the old greek parliament  today 24 ex-lawmakers are meeting here  to form a new parliament a parliament in  exile and a female one  before the taliban invasion there were  69 women lawmakers in afghanistan  now not a single woman is in government  and the legislature has been abolished  shagufa norzai starkly warns the west  over officially recognizing the  women taliban increase  intelligence will leave afghanistan  we will not have individual freedom  everyone who was busy with the religious  extremists i am  speaking from brussels member of the  european parliament hannah neumann  shares that view  dialogue with the taliban should  continue but the west should also take  this exiled parliament seriously  the lawmakers here will need such  backing to give a voice to people in  their homeland  afghans who could not leave the country  and now have to live under the taliban

